(function(){window.FP_LIB=window.FP_LIB||{reports:[]};var seen=new Set(window.FP_LIB.reports.map(function(r){return r.id;}));[{"id":"A156","date":"2026-11-04","cat":"Selección y valoración","title":"TENB · Stage 3 completo · Esperar precio","text":"# TENB: completed Stage 3, wait for price\n\nGood company, wait for price. Stage 3 complete; $47.03 needs materially stronger combined growth and margins than the central case. Central value $19.83; central sensitivity $16–25. No approved entry.\n\nResearch date: November 4, 2026 UTC. Scanner reference: November 3, 2026; $47.03. The scanner timestamp has no specified timezone. This is a completed review, not purchase approval. Stage 3.5 and an entry plan remain separate. No trade, order, new stop or authorized position size.\n\n# TENB: Stage 3 completed, wait for a better price\n\n**Research date: November 4, 2026. Evaluated price: $47.03, November 3 regular close, 4:00 p.m. EST.** USD throughout; financial tables in USD millions, shares in millions. Scanner Opportunity 90 and QP 100 prioritize research, not purchase authorization. The scanner's “Quote on 11/3/26 17:27:04” has no specified timezone. Its $47.03 happens to match the independently verified regular close [S8,S18].\n\n**Decision: Good company; wait for price. No Stage 3.5 approval and no trade.** The business is improving, but $47.03 already requires an unusually successful combination of growth and owner-cost margin expansion. Ten-year operating FCFF yields **$4.66 adverse, $19.83 central and $46.83 favorable**. A central operating sensitivity range is approximately **$16–25**; a stronger joint operating case reaches **$34**, so the fair assessment is not that $20 is an exact ceiling. The favorable case can support roughly today's price, but current evidence does not support treating that outcome as the central expectation. Scenarios are not probabilities or worst-case limits.\n\nThis is a completed Stage 3 review, superseding the November 2 bounded prioritization only. Confidence is high in “not attractive at today's price” and medium-low in a precise intrinsic value because the owner-margin recovery is young. **No universal 15% discount is imposed.** A reasonable-price partial could be evaluated under the plan if future evidence supports the price, but smaller size cannot repair today's expectation gap.\n\n## Earnings and source cut-off\n\nQ3 results were released **October 28, 2026, 4:05 p.m. EDT**, with the call at 4:30 p.m. The full September-quarter 10-Q was filed **November 3**, accession 0001660280-26-000054 [S2,S3,S15]. Thus this is post-earnings research, not a company with results still pending in November 2–6. The October 28 release and the newly filed 10-Q are both incorporated. No new results date is invented.\n\nThe full 10-Q, its filing date and its XBRL values were cross-checked. No essential-data blocker remains for this conclusion.\n\n## What the business is and what is improving\n\nTenable sells vulnerability and exposure management across IT, cloud, identities, operational technology and AI systems. Its installed sensors, vulnerability research and customer workflows create switching costs and opportunities to cross-sell the broader Tenable One platform. This is a credible franchise, not a broken-company thesis. The issuer reports over 40,000 customers and over 5,000 using Tenable One; the latter was **59% of Q3 new sales**, not 59% of revenue [S5].\n\nThe growth evidence is encouraging but does not yet prove a new 20% company-wide growth regime:\n\n- Q3 subscription revenue **$254.600m, +9.64%**; recurring revenue approximately **95%** of total. The company uses ARR in its retention calculation but does **not disclose a standalone total ARR balance/growth series in the reviewed filing and results**. Subscription growth and RPO must not be relabeled ARR growth.\n- Dollar-based net expansion **107%**, versus 106% in Q2 and 106% a year earlier. This measures the prior-year cohort including expansion/contraction/churn. It is not gross retention, and it does not independently establish pricing power.\n- Short-term RPO **$747.222m, +11.7%**; total RPO **$1,063.044m, +14.4%**. Longer duration partly explains total RPO growing faster than short-term RPO; neither is immediate revenue.\n- **413** new enterprise-platform logos; six-figure customers **2,275 versus 2,156**, +5.5%, with 39 net additions in Q3 [S2,S3,S5].\n\nAgainst July guidance, Q3 revenue beat the $271.5m midpoint by $4.887m, or 1.8%, and non-GAAP operating income beat $67.5m by $7.388m, or 10.9%. FY26 revenue midpoint rose **$1,078m to $1,085.5m**, only 0.7%; non-GAAP EBIT midpoint rose **$261m to $271.5m**, 4.0%. **Unlevered FCF guidance stayed $289–295m**, rather than being raised with earnings. Q4 revenue guidance of $277–280m implies about 6.9% growth at midpoint against Q4 2025. These facts support real operating leverage but caution against extrapolating EPS acceleration [S3,S4].\n\n## Financial record: three years, four quarters and TTM\n\nFYs and quarterly statements are sourced to SEC filings and companyfacts. Q4 2025 = FY2025 less nine months; Q2/Q3 cash flows are cumulative differences. TTM = Q4 2025 + Q1–Q3 2026. Prior TTM is the equivalent September 2025 period. Capex includes cash capitalized software [S1,S2,S15].\n\n| Period | Revenue | GAAP EBIT | Net income | CFO | Cash capex + software | Cash FCF | Expensed SBC |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| FY2023 | 798.71 | -52.16 | -78.28 | 149.85 | 8.76 | 141.10 | 145.33 |\n| FY2024 | 900.02 | -6.86 | -36.30 | 217.48 | 10.70 | 206.78 | 163.51 |\n| FY2025 | 999.40 | -9.17 | -36.12 | 266.75 | 16.58 | 250.17 | 191.81 |\n| Q42025 | 260.53 | 8.86 | -0.74 | 83.03 | 2.13 | 80.90 | 44.50 |\n| Q12026 | 262.06 | 8.76 | 1.41 | 87.97 | 5.33 | 82.64 | 43.85 |\n| Q22026 | 268.51 | 12.37 | 3.81 | 44.72 | 5.55 | 39.17 | 46.35 |\n| Q32026 | 276.39 | 16.71 | 11.30 | 72.52 | 8.29 | 64.22 | 51.22 |\n| TTM2025 | 974.60 | -5.05 | -33.51 | 264.84 | 17.29 | 247.55 | 188.02 |\n| TTM2026 | 1,067.49 | 46.70 | 15.78 | 288.23 | 21.31 | 266.93 | 185.92 |\n\nGAAP diluted EPS: FY2023 **−$0.68**, FY2024 **−$0.31**, FY2025 **−$0.30**; Q4 2025 **−$0.01**, Q1 2026 **$0.01**, Q2 **$0.03**, Q3 **$0.10**. Reported-quarter rounded EPS sum to $0.13, but that is not a new weighted-average share count. TTM net income/current diluted economic shares is separately about $0.14.\n\nTTM revenue grows **9.53%** and cash FCF **7.83%**. EBIT improves from **−$5.048m to +$46.695m** and net income from **−$33.513m to +$15.781m**. A percentage growth rate across a loss/near-zero base would mislead. GAAP EBIT margins progress from −6.5% in 2023 to −0.8% in 2024, −0.9% in 2025 and **4.4% TTM**, with **6.0% in Q3**. Gross margin is relatively steady, near 78%; improving expense leverage matters more than a gross-margin windfall.\n\n### Normalization: genuine progress, not free SBC\n\nThe prior TTM includes **$14.6m** from accelerated former-CEO equity vesting. Removing that discrete charge changes prior EBIT to **+$9.552m**; current EBIT is still $46.695m. Recovery is real even after removing the favorable comparison. Restructuring and acquisition charges are disclosed, not all automatically removed from a business that has repeatedly incurred them. Acquisition amortization stays within EBIT and is included once in D&A for the cash bridge.\n\nTTM expensed SBC **$185.922m** is **17.4% of revenue**. Q3 SBC rose **14.1%**, faster than revenue, to 18.5% of revenue. Thus no straight-line assumption that SBC quickly disappears is justified. Q3 non-GAAP EBIT $74.888m less SBC $51.217m is only $23.671m, or 8.56% of revenue, before restoring other excluded expenses. FY26 non-GAAP EBIT midpoint $271.5m less forecast SBC $185.3m leaves $86.2m, or 7.94%; restoring amortization/restructuring brings GAAP EBIT to **$55.5m**, or 5.11% [S3].\n\nSBC alone is not a veto. It is a recurring labor cost, and the DCF can value a business with it if margins and growth justify the price. This model keeps it in EBIT and does not additionally impose future dilution growth or hypothetical buyback spending.\n\n## Cash-quality reconciliation\n\nCash FCF is **CFO less PP&E less capitalized software**, not CFO alone. The company's “unlevered FCF” adds cash interest and financing costs without fully normalizing taxes, financial income, SBC or working capital; it is not the model's FCFF.\n\nTTM cash FCF $266.925m less expensed SBC $185.922m gives **$81.003m**, a useful owner-cost diagnostic, not formal FCFF. Additional capitalized SBC is approximately **$2.719m**, so a broader all-compensation diagnostic is **$78.284m**. At $4.915bn basic market equity this is only about **1.6%**.\n\nThe exact reported-to-normalized bridge is:\n\n1. Cash FCF **266.925**.\n2. Remove expensed SBC **−185.922**, giving **81.003**.\n3. Remove TTM reported working-capital contribution **−38.913** and other non-D&A noncash adjustments **−6.389**.\n4. Replace net income with EBIT taxed at 23%: **+20.174**, giving **55.875**.\n5. Include capitalized SBC as economic investment **−2.719**, giving **53.156m normalized operating FCFF before incremental working capital**.\n\nEquivalent formula: EBIT 46.695 ×77% + D&A 41.228 − cash capex 21.308 − capitalized SBC 2.719 =53.156. This bridge removes financial income and financing expense along with tax timing rather than counting them in operating value and cash twice. It is a normalization diagnostic, not a claim that cash taxes or working capital were actually zero.\n\nNine-month cash software investment rose **$2.676m to $11.712m**. Deferred commissions declined **$124.629m to $115.148m**: cash capitalized commissions of34.056m versus amortization 43.537m released 9.481m. The DCF does not perpetuate this release. Commission investment is included in forecast working capital, not also in PP&E capex. Revenue is predominantly USD-billed, reducing direct revenue FX exposure; international expenses still face FX risk.\n\nHistorical acquisitions consumed $243.3m/$29.2m/$196.2m in 2023/24/25. The forecast assumes growth from the already-owned business and expensed R&D, **no free future acquisitions**. A new acquisition requires deducting its price and reassessing its growth/margin contribution.\n\n## Balance sheet, ownership and convertible reconciliation\n\nSeptember cash plus short-term investments is **$495.261m**. Debt is **$800m principal**, not its $779.016m carrying amount. The operating-value-to-equity bridge subtracts $800m and adds $395.261m excess cash after a disclosed **$100m operating-liquidity reserve**, net **−$404.739m**. Releasing that entire reserve adds only $0.88 per modeled share. Operating lease payments remain inside operating costs, so $62.076m lease liabilities are not deducted a second time.\n\nThe convertible coupon is 0.25%, maturity **September 15, 2031**, conversion price about **$44.84**, and capped-call protection extends to **$64.06**. Principal must be settled in cash. Therefore the model subtracts $800m and does not also add the 17.84m gross underlying conversion shares. Above the cap, residual dilution is possible; all direct scenarios here are below it. Capped-call counterparty/early-conversion risks are real, not zero. At a $47.03 stock price, loss of the hedge's current intrinsic offset is only about $39m, or $0.35/share, but exposure grows with the stock. The separate $400m revolver was undrawn as of filing; it is liquidity, not excess cash [S2,S6,S7].\n\nOct 30 actual basic shares: **104.507623m**. The economic treasury-stock share bridge at $47.03 is:\n\n- Vested options 0.576m at $12.18: incremental **0.426825m**.\n- Unvested RSUs 14.436m less $328.966m remaining compensation/price: **7.441188m**.\n- PSUs 0.985m less $12.878m remaining compensation/price: **0.711175m**.\n- Total **113.086811m**. PSU realization is a target-based assumption. Unallocated plan shares are excluded.\n\nThis is a point-in-time economic approximation, not GAAP weighted shares. Remaining service compensation is treated as treasury-stock proceeds rather than counting every unvested share fully and charging all remaining compensation again. The model charges recurring compensation in future EBIT, keeps this denominator static and assumes no additional future share-count increase or free repurchases. For an even more generous countercheck, **basic shares only** produce central **$21.46** and favorable **$50.68**. Share-method uncertainty does not restore a central-value entry case.\n\nYTD cash repurchases of $454.0m exceed CFO $205.2m. EPS benefited from leverage-funded share reduction, not just operating growth. Nine-month equity grants also remain significant. Negative book equity chiefly reflects repurchases and historical losses; it is not itself proof of insolvency. Liquidity is adequate, but today's balance is no longer a net-cash balance sheet.\n\n## Ten-year FCFF model\n\nBase revenue is TTM **$1,067.486m**. Years 1–5 are explicit next-twelve-month periods, not mislabeled calendar-year guidance. Years 6–10 linearly transition growth and margin to terminal levels. Formula: **FCFF = EBIT ×(1−tax) + D&A − total investment − Δoperating working capital**. All forecast EBIT margins include SBC. No capitalization of expensed R&D is used to raise margins.\n\n| Input | Adverse | Central | Favorable |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue growth, years 1–5 | 6/5/4/3.5/3% | 10/10/9/8/7% | 14/14/13/12/10% |\n| EBIT margins, years 1–5 | 6.5/8/9/10/11% | 9/12/15/17.5/20% | 12/17/22/26/29% |\n| Year 10/terminal EBIT margin | 12% | 22% | 30% |\n| Normalized tax | 25% | 23% | 22% |\n| WACC | 11.5% | 10.5% | 9.5% |\n| Terminal growth | 2% | 2.5% | 3% |\n| Terminal incremental ROIC | 15% | 25% | 35% |\n| D&A/revenue years 1–5 | 3.5/3.2/2.9/2.6/2.3% | same | same |\n| Total capex/software/revenue years 1–5 | 3% | 3% | 3% |\n| Incremental WC/commission investment per extra revenue dollar | 3% | 2% | 1% |\n| Year 10 revenue | $1,483m | $2,007m | $2,558m |\n| Enterprise value | $931m | $2,648m | $5,701m |\n| Present equity value/share | **$4.66** | **$19.83** | **$46.83** |\n| Terminal share of enterprise value | 44.9% | 54.6% | 61.4% |\n\nThe central case is already an improvement case: current owner-cost margin is only 4.4% TTM/6.0% latest quarter. Reaching 22% needs considerable leverage while retaining investment and compensation. Qualys shows mature software margins above 30% are possible, but Tenable's product breadth, sales spending and acquisition footprint differ. The favorable case requires much stronger sales execution and cost leverage, not merely continuation of Q3.\n\nWACC anchor: the November 3 Federal Reserve H.15 release gives **4.91%** for the November 2 10-year Treasury. A modeled beta 1.2 and equity premium 5.5% imply cost of equity about 11.5%; 85% equity/15% debt, 6.5% economic debt cost and 23% tax imply about 10.5% WACC. These are underwriting assumptions, not a falsely precise measured beta or a claim the 0.25% convertible coupon is the economic cost of capital [S9].\n\nIn transition years, net investment/revenue glides from year 5 to the terminal requirement. **Terminal reinvestment = NOPAT ×g/ROIC**. The central terminal FCFF is 313.705m after 34.856m reinvestment. D&A is 2% in years 6–10; gross investment is solved so D&A, capex and ΔWC reconcile to the stated net requirement. All three cases satisfy WACC>g. This avoids an abrupt free-growth terminal.\n\nFederal/state/foreign NOLs and credits exist, but jurisdiction, ownership limits and expiry prevent treating the full face amount as freely spendable cash. The base uses normalized tax rates throughout and adds no separate tax asset. Even giving full undiscounted credit to the FY25 NOL deferred-tax asset plus tax credits would add less than **$2/share**, before limitations. This conservative omission cannot explain the roughly $27 price/base-value gap [S1].\n\n### Fair range and combined sensitivities\n\nHolding central operations fixed, WACC 9.5–11.5% and g2–3% produce **$16.42–24.75**. This is a sensitivity range around the central operating forecast, not the full adverse-to-favorable interval relabeled “fair.”\n\nJoint cases using 23% tax,2.5% terminal growth and 25% terminal ROIC show that the conclusion is not based on changing one variable at a time:\n\n| First-five-year annual growth | EBIT year 5/year 10 | WACC | Present value |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| 10% | 20%/22% | 10.5% | $21.95 |\n| 12% | 25%/27% | 10.0% | $34.03 |\n| 10% | 25%/27% | 9.5% | $32.77 |\n| 14% | 29%/30% | 9.5% | $47.44 |\n\nThe last combination roughly supports the market. It is feasible, not impossible, but asks growth to rise materially while operating margins rise from 6% to 29% in five years. The operating evidence presently supports a recovery, not sufficient confidence in that combination to call today's price reasonable.\n\n## Reverse valuation: alternative combinations\n\nUsing the same FCFF framework,10.5% WACC,2.5% terminal growth,23% tax and 25% terminal ROIC, $47.03 can be supported by these different paths. First-year margin 9% rises to year 5 margin; growth is flat for five years then fades over five years:\n\n| Growth each of first five years | EBIT year 5/year 10 | Revenue year 10 |\n|---|---|---:|\n| 22.55% | 20%/22% | $4,834m |\n| 18.75% | 25%/27% | $3,862m |\n| 15.68% | 30%/32% | $3,205m |\n| 13.82% | 35%/35% | $2,857m |\n\nFor illustration only, holding growth to 10% for five years requires roughly 43.7% operating margin by year 5 and thereafter. **That is not the sole expectation embedded in the market price.** Lower cost of capital and stronger joint margins/growth reduce each requirement, as shown above. Direct and reverse valuations are two views of one model, not independent confirmations.\n\n## Comparables: useful context, no mechanical averaging\n\nQuotes are November 3 closes. Balance sheets are latest available statements: TENB September, QLYS/RPD June. Market EV here uses **all liquid investments**, no discretionary operating-cash reserve, and principal debt consistently. Forward sales use company FY26 guidance midpoint, not a mixed consensus denominator. Market multiples do not replace the owner-cost DCF.\n\n| Company | Close | EV/FY26 sales | FY26 non-GAAP P/E | Latest quarterly growth | Latest GAAP EBIT margin |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| TENB | $47.03 | 4.81x | 21.62x | +9.5% | 6.0% |\n| QLYS | $210.57 | 8.95x | 26.96x | +11.0% | 34.0% |\n| RPD | $14.84 | 1.43x | 8.22x | −1.5% | 1.4% |\n\nTenable lies between a much more profitable compounder and a shrinking, more leveraged turnaround. Its sales discount to Qualys is partly justified by owner margins: TENB is about **111.8x TTM GAAP EBIT** versus QLYS **27.2x**. TENB's non-GAAP P/E hides much more SBC than GAAP EBIT. RPD has declining ARR and a $600m near-term convertible maturity within its roughly $900m debt; its low multiple is not a free-money benchmark. RPD's earnings are still upcoming, so no unreported Q3 result is imported [S10–S13,S16,S17].\n\nQualys and Rapid7 are closest product peers. CrowdStrike, Palo Alto Networks, hyperscalers/cloud platforms and open-source/internal tools are competitive threats but not clean valuation peers: growth, product mix and profit definitions differ. A security-platform consolidation cycle can expand Tenable One cross-sell and also strengthen bigger vendors' bundles. AI increases exposures and opportunity, but autonomous tooling can pressure prices or commoditize parts of scanning. No unsupported claim is made that AI guarantees either outcome.\n\n## Why the stock pulled back\n\nThe verified sequence is earnings October 28, close $48.72; October 29 close $51.78 (+6.28%); October 30 high $53.22; November 2 close $47.45 (−6.50%); November 3 close $47.03 (−0.89%). The high-to-close decline is **11.63%**. The recent retreat follows a large prior rally and occurs alongside peer weakness. The official release did not cut full-year revenue or profit guidance. Valuation digestion and sector de-risking are plausible interpretations, **not a proven issuer-specific cause**. No fabricated regulatory, customer-loss or earnings-warning catalyst is used [S3,S8,S12,S13].\n\n## Decision, triggers and risks\n\nThe Stage 3 business-quality gate passes: recurring demand, positive owner-cost cash, improving GAAP profit and adequate liquidity. **The valuation/expectations gate fails at $47.03.** This is a price decision, not rejection of the company or SBC as an automatic exclusion.\n\nA **$17–20 research-revisit zone** is a practical alert range around or modestly below today's central estimate. It is neither an authorized buy ceiling nor a standing order. At a genuinely well-supported reasonable value, the plan permits reviewing a partial position without an automatic 15% haircut. Price falling alone would not suffice if operations deteriorated. Conversely, verified improvement in growth, retention and margins can raise the valuation without requiring the market to fall to this range.\n\nReassess after FY26 results/guidance, a material change in financing or acquisitions, or major new operating evidence. Evidence that could support a higher central value: company-wide growth sustainably above 12%, retention reaching 110% with better large-account growth, GAAP margins improving without capitalized-cost or compensation distortions, and owner FCFF rising faster than revenue. These are research signposts, not new mechanical user rules.\n\nThesis risks/invalidation: sustained retention deterioration or outright cohort contraction; failure of Tenable One to translate bookings mix into aggregate growth; repeated dilution/repurchases financed by debt without owner-value growth; AI/cloud competition forcing investment or price cuts; adverse cyber or product-performance incidents; and new acquisitions whose returns fail to cover cost. A material financing issue, accounting contradiction or recovery reversal would move the verdict from price-wait to watch/reject. No such hard blocker was established in the reviewed statements.\n\n## Model and quality checks\n\nThe accompanying JSON and three ten-year CSVs expose every forecast year, tax, D&A, gross investment, working capital, net reinvestment, FCFF and present value. Four-quarter sums reconcile to TTM for all monetary fields. Direct and reverse share/debt assumptions match. Terminal reinvestment ties to g/ROIC. Debt is not counted with gross conversion shares; leases are not subtracted twice; SBC remains a cost with no additional future dilution assumption. The capitalized-SBC diagnostic is explicitly separate from cash capex. No trading authorization, fabricated approval, filled order or future event is recorded.\n\n## Sources\n- [S1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1660280/000166028026000005/tenb-20251231.htm)\n- [S2](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1660280/000166028026000054/tenb-20260930.htm)\n- [S3](https://investors.tenable.com/news-releases/news-release-details/tenable-announces-third-quarter-2026-financial-results/)\n- [S4](https://investors.tenable.com/news-releases/news-release-details/tenable-announces-second-quarter-2026-financial-results)\n- [S5](https://investors.tenable.com/static-files/9dc9ce3a-2f63-4757-958e-89eb24ce81d6)\n- [S6](https://investors.tenable.com/static-files/4c0cf94d-6b74-4b66-98d9-1e12311407ce)\n- [S7](https://investors.tenable.com/news-releases/news-release-details/tenable-announces-closing-new-400m-secured-revolving-credit)\n- [S8](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/tenb/history/)\n- [S9](https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/h15/)\n- [S10](https://investor.qualys.com/node/19341)\n- [S11](https://investors.rapid7.com/news/news-details/2026/Rapid7-Announces-Second-Quarter-2026-Financial-Results/default.aspx)\n- [S12](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/qlys/history/)\n- [S13](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/rpd/history/)\n- [S14](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/#plan)\n- [S15](https://data.sec.gov/api/xbrl/companyfacts/CIK0001660280.json)\n- [S16](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1107843/000110784326000035/qlys-20260630.htm)\n- [S17](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/rpd/)\n- [S18](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/data/scan-3960.js)\n\n\n## Full dossier and reproducible model\n\n- [Open the full TENB dossier](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/stocks/TENB.html)\n- [Download the full report](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/TENB/report.md)\n- [Model inputs and annual forecasts](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/TENB/model.json)\n- [Reproducible Python model](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/TENB/model.py)\n- [adverse 10year.csv](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/TENB/adverse_10year.csv)\n- [central 10year.csv](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/TENB/central_10year.csv)\n- [favorable 10year.csv](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/TENB/favorable_10year.csv)\n- [history.json](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/TENB/history.json)\n- [source registry.json](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/TENB/source_registry.json)\n","profiles":[{"ticker":"TENB","company":"Tenable Holdings, Inc.","kind":"complete","completedAt":"2026-11-04","label":"Stage 3 completo","summary":"Buena empresa, esperar precio. Stage 3 completo; 47,03 USD exige una combinación de crecimiento y márgenes mucho más favorable que el caso central. Valor central 19,83 USD; sensibilidad central 16–25 USD. No hay entrada aprobada.","summaryEn":"Good company, wait for price. Stage 3 complete; $47.03 needs materially stronger combined growth and margins than the central case. Central value $19.83; central sensitivity $16–25. No approved entry.","valuation":{"status":"conditional","currency":"USD","asOf":"2026-11-04","bear":4.66,"base":19.83,"bull":46.83,"label":"Modelo FCFF a 10 años · supuestos explícitos","labelEn":"10-year FCFF model · explicit assumptions"},"close":{"price":47.03,"sessionDate":"2026-11-03","priceType":"regular-close","currency":"USD","source":"https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/tenb/history/","note":"Cierre regular documentado en el informe; no cotización en vivo.","noteEn":"Regular close documented in the report; not a live quote."}}]},{"id":"A157","date":"2026-11-04","cat":"Selección y valoración","title":"RCL · Stage 3 completo · Esperar precio","text":"# RCL: completed Stage 3, wait for price\n\nStage 3 complete; wait for price. Strong operating quality, but $313.46 exceeds the $212.44 central case. The model funds ship commitments and separately values existing holdings and Sandals. No approved entry.\n\nResearch date: November 4, 2026 UTC. Scanner reference: November 3, 2026; $313.46. The scanner timestamp has no specified timezone. This is a completed review, not purchase approval. Stage 3.5 and an entry plan remain separate. No trade, order, new stop or authorized position size.\n\n# RCL and CCL: completed Fundamental Pullback Stage 3 reviews\n\nResearch cutoff: November 4, 2026, approximately 04:15 UTC. Reference prices are the November 3 scanner inputs supplied for this task, not live executable quotes: RCL $313.46; CCL $29.16. Opportunity score 90 is a screening input, not a valuation result. Dollar figures are US dollars; financial amounts are billions unless otherwise stated.\n\n## Decision\n\n**RCL: Stage 3 complete; do not select at the reference price under the stated return and reinvestment assumptions.** The business has better margins and substantial growth opportunities, but the price requires much more long-lived profitable growth than the base case. The Sandals acquisition and ship payments must be financed alongside existing claims. This is a valuation decision, not an incomplete-research label.\n\n**CCL: Stage 3 complete; retain as the nearer valuation watch candidate, but do not select at the reference price under the 10% base WACC.** It has a lower valuation and stronger near-term cash conversion, but that cash conversion benefits from a light delivery year. A 9% WACC nearly supports the scanner price; 10–11% does not. An investment case therefore needs explicit agreement on the required return and durable returns on new spending, rather than relying on the headline P/E.\n\nNeither conclusion implies a short recommendation, a trade, or a proven technical setup. Stage 3.5 requires separate, current market/sector conditions, actual trend and support, and an executable entry/stop/target. There is no universal 15% discount rule here.\n\n## Earnings gate and operating evidence\n\nBoth companies have already reported, so neither requires a deferral for this week's earnings.\n\n| Reported data | RCL | CCL |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Earnings release | October 27, 2026 | September 29, 2026 |\n| Quarter end | September 30 | August 31 |\n| Q3 revenue | 5.591 | 8.435 |\n| Q3 GAAP operating income | 1.871 | 2.220 |\n| Q3 adjusted EPS | $6.57 | $1.43 |\n| FY2026 adjusted EPS guidance | $17.85–17.95 | approximately $2.24 |\n| Q3 constant-currency net-yield growth | 0.6% | 2.4% |\n| 9M operating cash flow | 5.274 | 5.303 |\n| 9M cash capex | 3.880 | 2.139 |\n| OCF less capex, calculated | 1.394 | 3.164 |\n\nRCL's lower Q3 unit costs partly shifted into Q4. CCL's adjusted Q3 EBITDA was flat year over year despite beating guidance. In both cases, booking strength is encouraging, but a beat is not proof of accelerating underlying margins. Sources: [RCL Q3 release](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000088488726000042/a2026q3earningsrelease.htm), [CCL Q3 release](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/815097/000081509726000104/a20263qearningsrelease8-k.htm).\n\n### Seasonal and recurring normalization\n\nDo not multiply peak Q3 EPS, EBIT, EBITDA, or cash flow by four. Use nine months plus company Q4 guidance and then normalize capital needs.\n\n- **RCL starting recurring consolidated EBIT: 5.50**, analyst estimate. The bridge is approximately FY adjusted net income 4.81, plus recurring net interest 1.00, tax and minority earnings approximately 0.12, less existing equity-method investment income approximately 0.42 and small other items. The operating estimate is consistent with nine-month GAAP EBIT 4.34 and a roughly 1.15 fourth quarter. Excludes future Sandals earnings.\n- **CCL starting recurring EBIT: 4.22**, analyst estimate from FY adjusted EBITDA guidance 7.14 less depreciation/amortization 2.92. This is a full-year operating run rate, not Q3 annualization.\n- Base cash-tax assumption is **2%**, consistent with the currently low effective tax burden, not a universal US statutory rate. Explicit 5% and 10% tax sensitivities follow. Tax regimes and minimum-tax changes remain risks.\n- Stock compensation stays in operating expense. It is not added back to this FCFF model. Debt refinancing losses are excluded from recurring operating profit, but actual financing fees and capital obligations still matter.\n- Existing RCL equity-method holdings are valued separately from consolidated operating EBIT. Their accounting income is not treated as freely distributable cash.\n\n### Cash generation versus maintenance and growth spending\n\nThe nine-month OCF-minus-capex figures above are **after interest**, seasonal, and before discretionary distributions/debt repayment. They are not unlevered FCFF or money freely available to shareholders.\n\nA current full-year cash bridge, using analyst assumptions of 0.10 normalized working-capital investment, is:\n\n- RCL: 5.50 × 98% + 1.88 D&A − 4.60 capex − 0.10 working capital = **2.57 core FCFF**. Adding approximately 0.35 assumed net cash contribution from existing unconsolidated holdings gives approximately **2.92**, before the new Sandals investment.\n- CCL: 4.22 × 98% + 2.92 D&A − 3.339 capex − 0.10 working capital = **3.62 FCFF**. The 3.339 capex estimate is reported 9M purchases 2.139 plus the Q4 newbuild/non-newbuild outlook of 0.3/0.9. [CCL guidance presentation](https://www.carnivalcorp.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Third-Quarter-2026-Earnings-Presentation.pdf)\n\nNeither company discloses a clean economic maintenance-capex number. RCL's 1.5 non-new-ship spending is not synonymous with maintenance: it also covers destination and other investment. Similarly, CCL's non-newbuild spending mixes refurbishments, compliance, and destinations. Conversely, some future new vessels replace aging capacity rather than represent pure discretionary growth. The durable model must fund replacement, dry docks, environmental spending, and growth, rather than adding back all depreciation and deducting only a small maintenance proxy.\n\n## Debt, deposits, dilution, and other claims\n\n### RCL\n\nDebt principal including finance leases is **22.872**, not the 22.355 balance-sheet carrying amount after issuance-cost deductions. Cash is 0.851. Operating lease liability is 0.815. Debt maturities are 2.371 in 2027 and 3.211 in 2028. September debt cost averaged 4.71%; August unsecured financing carried 5.55%. The planned acquisition has a separate 3.0, 364-day facility, drawable at closing. [SEC debt note](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000088488726000044/R13.htm)\n\nCurrent deposits are 6.034. Diluted Q3 shares are 268m versus 274m a year earlier; actual September shares are approximately 267.47m. The model uses 268m and does not forecast free share-count shrinkage. Dividends and repurchases of approximately 2.11 exceeded nine-month OCF less capex. [RCL release](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000088488726000042/a2026q3earningsrelease.htm)\n\nThe orderbook costs approximately 19.4, with 1.5 deposited. The 2027–2030 ship-payment schedule used below is 2.532/4.918/3.605/2.066. Sources: [SEC commitments note](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000088488726000044/R15.htm), [Q3 filing](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000088488726000044/rcl-20260930.htm), material cash requirements section. These amounts constrain cash conversion even when financing is committed; borrowing does not make capital expenditure disappear.\n\n### CCL\n\nDebt principal is **24.559**, versus 23.912 carrying value. Cash is 1.220; operating leases total 1.250; total deposits are 7.639. 2027–2030 principal maturities are 2.517/3.961/3.631/2.885. New-ship commitments are 1.6/1.5/1.8/1.7 for those years, plus 11.4 thereafter. In December 2025 the convertible settlement issued **69.1m shares** and used 0.5 cash. The May 2026 dual-listing unification was one-for-one; do not add a separate legacy CUK share claim afterward. [CCL Q3 10-Q, debt, deposits and commitments](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/815097/000081509726000107/pdfofform10q.pdf)\n\nThe model uses the reported Q3 diluted 1.368bn shares, conservatively above Q4 guidance of 1.358bn. It does not credit announced buybacks without also funding them. Basic shares can rise following conversions while diluted shares fall, so one share-count series alone is misleading.\n\n### Consistent treatment\n\n- Deposits are operating liabilities, not excess cash or ordinary debt. They are captured through working capital and future voyage costs. Subtracting all deposits as debt after also modeling those obligations would double count. The model gives no recurring cash-flow bonus from rising deposit balances.\n- Analyst operating-cash reserves are 0.50 RCL and 0.75 CCL. Only the remaining 0.351/0.470 is credited as excess cash. These are assumptions, not disclosed minimum-cash requirements.\n- Core enterprise-to-equity claims are therefore **22.721 RCL** = 22.872 − 0.351 + 0.200 minority interest; **24.108 CCL** = 24.559 − 0.470 + 0.019 minority interest. Minority interests use carrying values as approximations.\n- Operating rent stays in EBIT/FCFF, so operating lease liabilities are not deducted again. Finance leases already included in debt are not added twice. A lease-capitalized alternative would require adjusting both cash flow and claims.\n- Ship capex is charged in forecast FCFF, not subtracted again as a lump-sum debt claim. Future borrowing is financing, not a source of FCFF. Likewise, don't both subtract current debt and deduct its principal amortization inside FCFF.\n- An unmodeled 1.0 additional equity-funded liability reduces value by approximately **$3.73 RCL / $0.73 CCL**, before any operating effect. Extra 10% dilution reduces per-share value approximately 9.1%, all else equal.\n\n### Pending Sandals acquisition\n\nRCL agreed to buy 50% for approximately 3.0 in cash; closing is expected in early 2027. This is a material **future funded capital claim**, not September debt already outstanding. [September 23 acquisition filing](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000110465926109755/tm2625963d1_8k.htm)\n\nBase SOTP treatment credits the acquired stake at its 3.0 cost and deducts the matching 3.0 funding claim, for zero incremental net value. This is not a claim that intrinsic value has been independently proved. The bear case assumes a 0.75 loss of value; the bull assumes 1.0 value creation. Neither adds Sandals EBITDA or earnings to consolidated core EBIT. This avoids counting acquired profit without the purchase cost or subtracting the cost without any acquired asset. Permanent financing, cash distributions, resort upkeep, and incremental capital requests are the next key checks.\n\nExisting RCL unconsolidated holdings are a separate 4.75 base equity value: assumed net distributions 0.35 growing 5% for five years, 3% for five years, then 2.5%, discounted at 11% because these are equity distributions. Do not subtract their already-serviced JV debt again or add their accounting earnings to core EBIT. This is an assumption-driven valuation, with 3.8/6.0 bear/bull values.\n\n## Discount rate and 10-year model\n\nThe official November 3 Treasury 10-year yield is **4.95%**. [Treasury daily rates](https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?field_tdr_date_value=2026&type=daily_treasury_yield_curve)\n\nUnderwriting assumptions, not claimed observed regression estimates: ERP 4.5%; levered beta 1.35 RCL/1.50 CCL; marginal debt costs 6.0%/6.5%; 2% debt tax shield. At approximate market-value capital weights these indicate roughly 10% WACC. Historical coupons are not current marginal yields. Use **9%, 10%, and 11%** rather than false precision. No consensus estimates are substituted for these assumptions.\n\nCore model years are 2027–2036, end-year discounted:\n\n1. NOPAT = recurring consolidated EBIT × (1 − cash tax).\n2. Economic net reinvestment = NOPAT × growth / incremental ROIC.\n3. In 2027–2030 use the greater of economic net reinvestment and a ship-payment floor: contracted newbuild cash payments + assumed other capex − modeled D&A + 0.10 working capital. This is a floor, not an additional second reinvestment deduction.\n4. Terminal FCFF = year-10 NOPAT × (1 + terminal growth) × (1 − terminal growth / terminal ROIC).\n5. Equity = core EV + existing-JV equity value + net acquired value − net capital claims; divide by diluted shares.\n\nThe capex-floor assumptions use RCL other capex 1.5 growing 3%, D&A 2.0/2.1/2.2/2.3; CCL other capex 2.7 growing 3%, D&A 3.0/3.1/3.2/3.3. Combined with disclosed ship commitments these create net-reinvestment floors:\n\n| 2027–2030 | 2027 | 2028 | 2029 | 2030 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| RCL | 2.132 | 4.463 | 3.096 | 1.505 |\n| CCL | 1.400 | 1.281 | 1.564 | 1.450 |\n\n## Direct valuation: complete assumptions and outputs\n\n| Assumption | RCL bear | RCL base | RCL bull | CCL bear | CCL base | CCL bull |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Starting EBIT | 4.95 | 5.50 | 5.50 | 3.80 | 4.22 | 4.22 |\n| Tax | 4% | 2% | 2% | 4% | 2% | 2% |\n| NOPAT growth years 1–5 | 3% | 9% | 12% | 2% | 7% | 10% |\n| NOPAT growth years 6–10 | 3% | 5% | 7% | 2% | 4% | 6% |\n| Incremental ROIC years 1–5 | 15% | 25% | 30% | 15% | 25% | 30% |\n| Incremental ROIC years 6–10 | 12% | 18% | 22% | 12% | 18% | 22% |\n| WACC | 11% | 10% | 9% | 11% | 10% | 9% |\n| Terminal growth | 2% | 2.5% | 3% | 2% | 2.5% | 3% |\n| Terminal ROIC | 9% | 12% | 15% | 9% | 12% | 15% |\n| Value per share | **$73** | **$212** | **$364** | **$5** | **$22** | **$41** |\n\nThe base incremental returns are deliberately favorable, reflecting pricing, mix, operating leverage, and destination contribution rather than treating every dollar of earnings growth as coming from a newly purchased vessel. They are not company guidance or guaranteed fleet returns. Long-term return fade and committed capex prevent those favorable assumptions from becoming free growth.\n\nBear values are long-duration stress valuations, not forecasts of a near-term trading low, liquidation value, or stop. No scenario probabilities are assigned. The large equity downside is the consequence of fixed debt claims against a lower enterprise valuation.\n\nBase core annual FCFF, years 1–10:\n\n- RCL: **3.743, 1.941, 3.884, 4.869, 5.308, 6.289, 6.603, 6.934, 7.280, 7.644**. The 2028 dip reflects contracted ship payments. Core EV 74.906 = explicit PV 30.754 + terminal PV 44.152. Add existing JV equity 4.750; subtract claims 22.721; divide by 0.268bn shares = $212.44. Terminal share of core EV is 58.9%.\n- CCL: **3.025, 3.409, 3.502, 3.903, 4.176, 4.692, 4.880, 5.075, 5.278, 5.489**. Core EV 54.769 = explicit PV 25.332 + terminal PV 29.438. Subtract claims 24.108; divide by 1.368bn shares = $22.41. Terminal share is 53.7%.\n\nThese are current intrinsic values, not year-10 stock-price targets. Don't append ten years of dividends to them; the cash flows are already capitalized. The high terminal weights explain why growth, reinvestment and discount-rate sensitivity are essential.\n\n### WACC / terminal-growth sensitivity\n\nAll other base inputs fixed; terminal ROIC remains 12% and terminal reinvestment is recalculated at each growth rate.\n\n| RCL per share | g 2.0% | g 2.5% | g 3.0% |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| WACC 9% | $256 | $262 | $269 |\n| WACC 10% | $209 | $212 | $216 |\n| WACC 11% | $173 | $175 | $176 |\n\n| CCL per share | g 2.0% | g 2.5% | g 3.0% |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| WACC 9% | $28.27 | $29.02 | $29.87 |\n| WACC 10% | $22.03 | $22.41 | $22.84 |\n| WACC 11% | $17.20 | $17.38 | $17.58 |\n\nAdditional sensitivities:\n\n- Increasing cash tax to 5%/10%: RCL **$203/$187**; CCL **$21.13/$18.93**.\n- Reducing incremental ROIC to 20%/15% across the two five-year phases: RCL **$202**; CCL **$21.13**.\n- Reducing incremental ROIC to 15%/12%: RCL **$184**; CCL **$18.76**.\n- The base values assume no future net dilution. Ten percent additional dilution gives approximately **$193/$20.38**.\n\n## Reverse valuation: what today's price asks the business to deliver\n\nSolve for constant ten-year NOPAT growth, using **25% incremental ROIC for all ten years**, a favorable sustained reinvestment assumption; retain commitment floors, claims, 2.5% terminal growth and 12% terminal ROIC. This is an alternative reverse case, not the same fading-ROIC base case.\n\n| Required ten-year NOPAT CAGR | WACC 9% | WACC 10% | WACC 11% |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| RCL at $313.46 | **9.4%** | **12.4%** | **15.5%** |\n| CCL at $29.16 | **5.7%** | **8.4%** | **11.4%** |\n\nAt 10% WACC, the required year-10 NOPAT is approximately **17.31 RCL** and **9.26 CCL**, versus starting 5.39/4.136. If the model's base growth/return path is held fixed instead, current price requires starting recurring EBIT approximately **7.35 RCL** or **4.91 CCL**, above the normalized 5.50/4.22 anchors.\n\nInterpretation: the market is not merely valuing today's cruises. RCL needs substantial sustained platform growth and high returns on expanding capacity. CCL's implied bar is lower, but a continuation of mid-single-digit EBIT growth alone does not support $29 at a 10% discount rate unless capital efficiency or starting profit also improves.\n\n## Sector comparison and thesis tests\n\nAt the supplied prices, RCL is approximately **17.5×** FY adjusted EPS versus **13.0×** for CCL. Approximate enterprise-value/adjusted-EBITDA is **13.6× RCL / 8.9× CCL**, using gross principal less reported cash and approximately 7.79/7.14 FY EBITDA. RCL's EBITDA estimate is analyst-derived and includes equity-method contribution, so this headline comparison is a cross-check, not a replacement for the separate-JV DCF. Their corporate ROIC definitions also differ and should not be used as directly comparable incremental returns.\n\nOperational quality ranking is RCL, then CCL, then turnaround-risk NCLH; valuation attractiveness between the two covered names favors CCL. This is an analytical judgment, not a universal sector ranking. NCLH reported Q3 constant-currency net yields down 6.7%, FY EBITDA guidance 2.55, net debt 14.8, reported net leverage 5.5×, and October 8.75% refinancing. Its bookings remain below the desired range. It is not a clean quality/multiple anchor for either company's premium. [NCLH Q3 company release](https://www.nclhltd.com/news-media/press-releases/detail/835/norwegian-cruise-line-holdings-reports-third-quarter-2026)\n\n**What would strengthen RCL:** same-ship yield growth after timing effects; destination and new-fleet earnings that convert to cash after upkeep; existing JV distributions; Sandals earning an adequate after-capex return on its funded cost; and capex/debt staying within the modeled envelope. **What would weaken it:** price-led growth giving way to discounting, capex overruns, a heavier resort funding requirement, or buybacks masking weak aggregate cash conversion.\n\n**What would strengthen CCL:** same-ship yield and onboard growth exceeding unit-cost inflation, delivery-year capex remaining controlled, sustained debt reduction after dividends/buybacks, and margin expansion sufficient to clear the 8.4% reverse growth bar without years of unusually high incremental returns. **What would weaken it:** the cash-flow advantage disappearing when deliveries resume, deposit growth reversing, or earnings per share improving mainly through refinancing/share-count effects.\n\nFor both, fuel, currency, refunds, environmental/port charges, tax changes, destination concentration, consumer spending and shipyard timing remain material. Deposits create funding resilience in normal conditions but can reverse during cancellations; a balance-sheet claim and cash-flow stress must not be counted twice.\n\n## Stage 3 versus Stage 3.5\n\nThe completed fundamental work does not clear either name at the quoted price under the base return hurdle. CCL deserves the earlier re-underwriting check because its value reaches the quote around a 9% WACC; RCL remains above the shown base sensitivities even there. Lower quotes or stronger evidenced cash economics can change either conclusion. No fixed discount percentage is imposed.\n\nA valid technical 3:1 uses **(credible target − actual entry) / (actual entry − technically justified stop)** and incorporates execution/slippage risk. It does not use this DCF's bear scenario as a stop, and a 3:1 chart setup cannot by itself resolve a failed fundamental valuation. Verify current market/sector conditions and price structure separately before considering promotion or a trade.\n\n## Reproducibility\n\nThe accompanying Python model and JSON contain every numerical assumption, each annual cash flow, each present value, and the full claim bridge. No trades were placed. Source and model uncertainty are disclosed as assumptions rather than being called unfinished research. Formal market data/consensus feeds were not used; price references are explicitly scanner-supplied.\n\n\n## Full dossier and reproducible model\n\n- [Open the full RCL dossier](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/stocks/RCL.html)\n- [Download the full report](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/RCL/report.md)\n- [Model inputs and annual forecasts](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/RCL/model.json)\n- [Reproducible Python model](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/RCL/model.py)\n","profiles":[{"ticker":"RCL","company":"Royal Caribbean Group","kind":"complete","completedAt":"2026-11-04","label":"Stage 3 completo","summary":"Stage 3 completo; esperar precio. Buena calidad operativa, pero 313,46 USD supera el central 212,44 USD. El modelo financia compromisos de flota y separa las participaciones y Sandals. No hay entrada aprobada.","summaryEn":"Stage 3 complete; wait for price. Strong operating quality, but $313.46 exceeds the $212.44 central case. The model funds ship commitments and separately values existing holdings and Sandals. No approved entry.","valuation":{"status":"conditional","currency":"USD","asOf":"2026-11-04","bear":73.3,"base":212.44,"bull":363.83,"label":"Modelo FCFF a 10 años · supuestos explícitos","labelEn":"10-year FCFF model · explicit assumptions"}}]},{"id":"A158","date":"2026-11-04","cat":"Selección y valoración","title":"CCL · Stage 3 completo · Esperar precio","text":"# CCL: completed Stage 3, wait for price\n\nStage 3 complete; wait for price. The nearer valuation watch candidate, but $29.16 exceeds the $22.41 central case at 10% WACC. At 9% it nearly supports the price: that return hurdle and reinvestment need explicit underwriting. No approved entry.\n\nResearch date: November 4, 2026 UTC. Scanner reference: November 3, 2026; $29.16. The scanner timestamp has no specified timezone. This is a completed review, not purchase approval. Stage 3.5 and an entry plan remain separate. No trade, order, new stop or authorized position size.\n\n# RCL and CCL: completed Fundamental Pullback Stage 3 reviews\n\nResearch cutoff: November 4, 2026, approximately 04:15 UTC. Reference prices are the November 3 scanner inputs supplied for this task, not live executable quotes: RCL $313.46; CCL $29.16. Opportunity score 90 is a screening input, not a valuation result. Dollar figures are US dollars; financial amounts are billions unless otherwise stated.\n\n## Decision\n\n**RCL: Stage 3 complete; do not select at the reference price under the stated return and reinvestment assumptions.** The business has better margins and substantial growth opportunities, but the price requires much more long-lived profitable growth than the base case. The Sandals acquisition and ship payments must be financed alongside existing claims. This is a valuation decision, not an incomplete-research label.\n\n**CCL: Stage 3 complete; retain as the nearer valuation watch candidate, but do not select at the reference price under the 10% base WACC.** It has a lower valuation and stronger near-term cash conversion, but that cash conversion benefits from a light delivery year. A 9% WACC nearly supports the scanner price; 10–11% does not. An investment case therefore needs explicit agreement on the required return and durable returns on new spending, rather than relying on the headline P/E.\n\nNeither conclusion implies a short recommendation, a trade, or a proven technical setup. Stage 3.5 requires separate, current market/sector conditions, actual trend and support, and an executable entry/stop/target. There is no universal 15% discount rule here.\n\n## Earnings gate and operating evidence\n\nBoth companies have already reported, so neither requires a deferral for this week's earnings.\n\n| Reported data | RCL | CCL |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Earnings release | October 27, 2026 | September 29, 2026 |\n| Quarter end | September 30 | August 31 |\n| Q3 revenue | 5.591 | 8.435 |\n| Q3 GAAP operating income | 1.871 | 2.220 |\n| Q3 adjusted EPS | $6.57 | $1.43 |\n| FY2026 adjusted EPS guidance | $17.85–17.95 | approximately $2.24 |\n| Q3 constant-currency net-yield growth | 0.6% | 2.4% |\n| 9M operating cash flow | 5.274 | 5.303 |\n| 9M cash capex | 3.880 | 2.139 |\n| OCF less capex, calculated | 1.394 | 3.164 |\n\nRCL's lower Q3 unit costs partly shifted into Q4. CCL's adjusted Q3 EBITDA was flat year over year despite beating guidance. In both cases, booking strength is encouraging, but a beat is not proof of accelerating underlying margins. Sources: [RCL Q3 release](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000088488726000042/a2026q3earningsrelease.htm), [CCL Q3 release](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/815097/000081509726000104/a20263qearningsrelease8-k.htm).\n\n### Seasonal and recurring normalization\n\nDo not multiply peak Q3 EPS, EBIT, EBITDA, or cash flow by four. Use nine months plus company Q4 guidance and then normalize capital needs.\n\n- **RCL starting recurring consolidated EBIT: 5.50**, analyst estimate. The bridge is approximately FY adjusted net income 4.81, plus recurring net interest 1.00, tax and minority earnings approximately 0.12, less existing equity-method investment income approximately 0.42 and small other items. The operating estimate is consistent with nine-month GAAP EBIT 4.34 and a roughly 1.15 fourth quarter. Excludes future Sandals earnings.\n- **CCL starting recurring EBIT: 4.22**, analyst estimate from FY adjusted EBITDA guidance 7.14 less depreciation/amortization 2.92. This is a full-year operating run rate, not Q3 annualization.\n- Base cash-tax assumption is **2%**, consistent with the currently low effective tax burden, not a universal US statutory rate. Explicit 5% and 10% tax sensitivities follow. Tax regimes and minimum-tax changes remain risks.\n- Stock compensation stays in operating expense. It is not added back to this FCFF model. Debt refinancing losses are excluded from recurring operating profit, but actual financing fees and capital obligations still matter.\n- Existing RCL equity-method holdings are valued separately from consolidated operating EBIT. Their accounting income is not treated as freely distributable cash.\n\n### Cash generation versus maintenance and growth spending\n\nThe nine-month OCF-minus-capex figures above are **after interest**, seasonal, and before discretionary distributions/debt repayment. They are not unlevered FCFF or money freely available to shareholders.\n\nA current full-year cash bridge, using analyst assumptions of 0.10 normalized working-capital investment, is:\n\n- RCL: 5.50 × 98% + 1.88 D&A − 4.60 capex − 0.10 working capital = **2.57 core FCFF**. Adding approximately 0.35 assumed net cash contribution from existing unconsolidated holdings gives approximately **2.92**, before the new Sandals investment.\n- CCL: 4.22 × 98% + 2.92 D&A − 3.339 capex − 0.10 working capital = **3.62 FCFF**. The 3.339 capex estimate is reported 9M purchases 2.139 plus the Q4 newbuild/non-newbuild outlook of 0.3/0.9. [CCL guidance presentation](https://www.carnivalcorp.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Third-Quarter-2026-Earnings-Presentation.pdf)\n\nNeither company discloses a clean economic maintenance-capex number. RCL's 1.5 non-new-ship spending is not synonymous with maintenance: it also covers destination and other investment. Similarly, CCL's non-newbuild spending mixes refurbishments, compliance, and destinations. Conversely, some future new vessels replace aging capacity rather than represent pure discretionary growth. The durable model must fund replacement, dry docks, environmental spending, and growth, rather than adding back all depreciation and deducting only a small maintenance proxy.\n\n## Debt, deposits, dilution, and other claims\n\n### RCL\n\nDebt principal including finance leases is **22.872**, not the 22.355 balance-sheet carrying amount after issuance-cost deductions. Cash is 0.851. Operating lease liability is 0.815. Debt maturities are 2.371 in 2027 and 3.211 in 2028. September debt cost averaged 4.71%; August unsecured financing carried 5.55%. The planned acquisition has a separate 3.0, 364-day facility, drawable at closing. [SEC debt note](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000088488726000044/R13.htm)\n\nCurrent deposits are 6.034. Diluted Q3 shares are 268m versus 274m a year earlier; actual September shares are approximately 267.47m. The model uses 268m and does not forecast free share-count shrinkage. Dividends and repurchases of approximately 2.11 exceeded nine-month OCF less capex. [RCL release](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000088488726000042/a2026q3earningsrelease.htm)\n\nThe orderbook costs approximately 19.4, with 1.5 deposited. The 2027–2030 ship-payment schedule used below is 2.532/4.918/3.605/2.066. Sources: [SEC commitments note](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000088488726000044/R15.htm), [Q3 filing](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000088488726000044/rcl-20260930.htm), material cash requirements section. These amounts constrain cash conversion even when financing is committed; borrowing does not make capital expenditure disappear.\n\n### CCL\n\nDebt principal is **24.559**, versus 23.912 carrying value. Cash is 1.220; operating leases total 1.250; total deposits are 7.639. 2027–2030 principal maturities are 2.517/3.961/3.631/2.885. New-ship commitments are 1.6/1.5/1.8/1.7 for those years, plus 11.4 thereafter. In December 2025 the convertible settlement issued **69.1m shares** and used 0.5 cash. The May 2026 dual-listing unification was one-for-one; do not add a separate legacy CUK share claim afterward. [CCL Q3 10-Q, debt, deposits and commitments](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/815097/000081509726000107/pdfofform10q.pdf)\n\nThe model uses the reported Q3 diluted 1.368bn shares, conservatively above Q4 guidance of 1.358bn. It does not credit announced buybacks without also funding them. Basic shares can rise following conversions while diluted shares fall, so one share-count series alone is misleading.\n\n### Consistent treatment\n\n- Deposits are operating liabilities, not excess cash or ordinary debt. They are captured through working capital and future voyage costs. Subtracting all deposits as debt after also modeling those obligations would double count. The model gives no recurring cash-flow bonus from rising deposit balances.\n- Analyst operating-cash reserves are 0.50 RCL and 0.75 CCL. Only the remaining 0.351/0.470 is credited as excess cash. These are assumptions, not disclosed minimum-cash requirements.\n- Core enterprise-to-equity claims are therefore **22.721 RCL** = 22.872 − 0.351 + 0.200 minority interest; **24.108 CCL** = 24.559 − 0.470 + 0.019 minority interest. Minority interests use carrying values as approximations.\n- Operating rent stays in EBIT/FCFF, so operating lease liabilities are not deducted again. Finance leases already included in debt are not added twice. A lease-capitalized alternative would require adjusting both cash flow and claims.\n- Ship capex is charged in forecast FCFF, not subtracted again as a lump-sum debt claim. Future borrowing is financing, not a source of FCFF. Likewise, don't both subtract current debt and deduct its principal amortization inside FCFF.\n- An unmodeled 1.0 additional equity-funded liability reduces value by approximately **$3.73 RCL / $0.73 CCL**, before any operating effect. Extra 10% dilution reduces per-share value approximately 9.1%, all else equal.\n\n### Pending Sandals acquisition\n\nRCL agreed to buy 50% for approximately 3.0 in cash; closing is expected in early 2027. This is a material **future funded capital claim**, not September debt already outstanding. [September 23 acquisition filing](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/884887/000110465926109755/tm2625963d1_8k.htm)\n\nBase SOTP treatment credits the acquired stake at its 3.0 cost and deducts the matching 3.0 funding claim, for zero incremental net value. This is not a claim that intrinsic value has been independently proved. The bear case assumes a 0.75 loss of value; the bull assumes 1.0 value creation. Neither adds Sandals EBITDA or earnings to consolidated core EBIT. This avoids counting acquired profit without the purchase cost or subtracting the cost without any acquired asset. Permanent financing, cash distributions, resort upkeep, and incremental capital requests are the next key checks.\n\nExisting RCL unconsolidated holdings are a separate 4.75 base equity value: assumed net distributions 0.35 growing 5% for five years, 3% for five years, then 2.5%, discounted at 11% because these are equity distributions. Do not subtract their already-serviced JV debt again or add their accounting earnings to core EBIT. This is an assumption-driven valuation, with 3.8/6.0 bear/bull values.\n\n## Discount rate and 10-year model\n\nThe official November 3 Treasury 10-year yield is **4.95%**. [Treasury daily rates](https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?field_tdr_date_value=2026&type=daily_treasury_yield_curve)\n\nUnderwriting assumptions, not claimed observed regression estimates: ERP 4.5%; levered beta 1.35 RCL/1.50 CCL; marginal debt costs 6.0%/6.5%; 2% debt tax shield. At approximate market-value capital weights these indicate roughly 10% WACC. Historical coupons are not current marginal yields. Use **9%, 10%, and 11%** rather than false precision. No consensus estimates are substituted for these assumptions.\n\nCore model years are 2027–2036, end-year discounted:\n\n1. NOPAT = recurring consolidated EBIT × (1 − cash tax).\n2. Economic net reinvestment = NOPAT × growth / incremental ROIC.\n3. In 2027–2030 use the greater of economic net reinvestment and a ship-payment floor: contracted newbuild cash payments + assumed other capex − modeled D&A + 0.10 working capital. This is a floor, not an additional second reinvestment deduction.\n4. Terminal FCFF = year-10 NOPAT × (1 + terminal growth) × (1 − terminal growth / terminal ROIC).\n5. Equity = core EV + existing-JV equity value + net acquired value − net capital claims; divide by diluted shares.\n\nThe capex-floor assumptions use RCL other capex 1.5 growing 3%, D&A 2.0/2.1/2.2/2.3; CCL other capex 2.7 growing 3%, D&A 3.0/3.1/3.2/3.3. Combined with disclosed ship commitments these create net-reinvestment floors:\n\n| 2027–2030 | 2027 | 2028 | 2029 | 2030 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| RCL | 2.132 | 4.463 | 3.096 | 1.505 |\n| CCL | 1.400 | 1.281 | 1.564 | 1.450 |\n\n## Direct valuation: complete assumptions and outputs\n\n| Assumption | RCL bear | RCL base | RCL bull | CCL bear | CCL base | CCL bull |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Starting EBIT | 4.95 | 5.50 | 5.50 | 3.80 | 4.22 | 4.22 |\n| Tax | 4% | 2% | 2% | 4% | 2% | 2% |\n| NOPAT growth years 1–5 | 3% | 9% | 12% | 2% | 7% | 10% |\n| NOPAT growth years 6–10 | 3% | 5% | 7% | 2% | 4% | 6% |\n| Incremental ROIC years 1–5 | 15% | 25% | 30% | 15% | 25% | 30% |\n| Incremental ROIC years 6–10 | 12% | 18% | 22% | 12% | 18% | 22% |\n| WACC | 11% | 10% | 9% | 11% | 10% | 9% |\n| Terminal growth | 2% | 2.5% | 3% | 2% | 2.5% | 3% |\n| Terminal ROIC | 9% | 12% | 15% | 9% | 12% | 15% |\n| Value per share | **$73** | **$212** | **$364** | **$5** | **$22** | **$41** |\n\nThe base incremental returns are deliberately favorable, reflecting pricing, mix, operating leverage, and destination contribution rather than treating every dollar of earnings growth as coming from a newly purchased vessel. They are not company guidance or guaranteed fleet returns. Long-term return fade and committed capex prevent those favorable assumptions from becoming free growth.\n\nBear values are long-duration stress valuations, not forecasts of a near-term trading low, liquidation value, or stop. No scenario probabilities are assigned. The large equity downside is the consequence of fixed debt claims against a lower enterprise valuation.\n\nBase core annual FCFF, years 1–10:\n\n- RCL: **3.743, 1.941, 3.884, 4.869, 5.308, 6.289, 6.603, 6.934, 7.280, 7.644**. The 2028 dip reflects contracted ship payments. Core EV 74.906 = explicit PV 30.754 + terminal PV 44.152. Add existing JV equity 4.750; subtract claims 22.721; divide by 0.268bn shares = $212.44. Terminal share of core EV is 58.9%.\n- CCL: **3.025, 3.409, 3.502, 3.903, 4.176, 4.692, 4.880, 5.075, 5.278, 5.489**. Core EV 54.769 = explicit PV 25.332 + terminal PV 29.438. Subtract claims 24.108; divide by 1.368bn shares = $22.41. Terminal share is 53.7%.\n\nThese are current intrinsic values, not year-10 stock-price targets. Don't append ten years of dividends to them; the cash flows are already capitalized. The high terminal weights explain why growth, reinvestment and discount-rate sensitivity are essential.\n\n### WACC / terminal-growth sensitivity\n\nAll other base inputs fixed; terminal ROIC remains 12% and terminal reinvestment is recalculated at each growth rate.\n\n| RCL per share | g 2.0% | g 2.5% | g 3.0% |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| WACC 9% | $256 | $262 | $269 |\n| WACC 10% | $209 | $212 | $216 |\n| WACC 11% | $173 | $175 | $176 |\n\n| CCL per share | g 2.0% | g 2.5% | g 3.0% |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| WACC 9% | $28.27 | $29.02 | $29.87 |\n| WACC 10% | $22.03 | $22.41 | $22.84 |\n| WACC 11% | $17.20 | $17.38 | $17.58 |\n\nAdditional sensitivities:\n\n- Increasing cash tax to 5%/10%: RCL **$203/$187**; CCL **$21.13/$18.93**.\n- Reducing incremental ROIC to 20%/15% across the two five-year phases: RCL **$202**; CCL **$21.13**.\n- Reducing incremental ROIC to 15%/12%: RCL **$184**; CCL **$18.76**.\n- The base values assume no future net dilution. Ten percent additional dilution gives approximately **$193/$20.38**.\n\n## Reverse valuation: what today's price asks the business to deliver\n\nSolve for constant ten-year NOPAT growth, using **25% incremental ROIC for all ten years**, a favorable sustained reinvestment assumption; retain commitment floors, claims, 2.5% terminal growth and 12% terminal ROIC. This is an alternative reverse case, not the same fading-ROIC base case.\n\n| Required ten-year NOPAT CAGR | WACC 9% | WACC 10% | WACC 11% |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| RCL at $313.46 | **9.4%** | **12.4%** | **15.5%** |\n| CCL at $29.16 | **5.7%** | **8.4%** | **11.4%** |\n\nAt 10% WACC, the required year-10 NOPAT is approximately **17.31 RCL** and **9.26 CCL**, versus starting 5.39/4.136. If the model's base growth/return path is held fixed instead, current price requires starting recurring EBIT approximately **7.35 RCL** or **4.91 CCL**, above the normalized 5.50/4.22 anchors.\n\nInterpretation: the market is not merely valuing today's cruises. RCL needs substantial sustained platform growth and high returns on expanding capacity. CCL's implied bar is lower, but a continuation of mid-single-digit EBIT growth alone does not support $29 at a 10% discount rate unless capital efficiency or starting profit also improves.\n\n## Sector comparison and thesis tests\n\nAt the supplied prices, RCL is approximately **17.5×** FY adjusted EPS versus **13.0×** for CCL. Approximate enterprise-value/adjusted-EBITDA is **13.6× RCL / 8.9× CCL**, using gross principal less reported cash and approximately 7.79/7.14 FY EBITDA. RCL's EBITDA estimate is analyst-derived and includes equity-method contribution, so this headline comparison is a cross-check, not a replacement for the separate-JV DCF. Their corporate ROIC definitions also differ and should not be used as directly comparable incremental returns.\n\nOperational quality ranking is RCL, then CCL, then turnaround-risk NCLH; valuation attractiveness between the two covered names favors CCL. This is an analytical judgment, not a universal sector ranking. NCLH reported Q3 constant-currency net yields down 6.7%, FY EBITDA guidance 2.55, net debt 14.8, reported net leverage 5.5×, and October 8.75% refinancing. Its bookings remain below the desired range. It is not a clean quality/multiple anchor for either company's premium. [NCLH Q3 company release](https://www.nclhltd.com/news-media/press-releases/detail/835/norwegian-cruise-line-holdings-reports-third-quarter-2026)\n\n**What would strengthen RCL:** same-ship yield growth after timing effects; destination and new-fleet earnings that convert to cash after upkeep; existing JV distributions; Sandals earning an adequate after-capex return on its funded cost; and capex/debt staying within the modeled envelope. **What would weaken it:** price-led growth giving way to discounting, capex overruns, a heavier resort funding requirement, or buybacks masking weak aggregate cash conversion.\n\n**What would strengthen CCL:** same-ship yield and onboard growth exceeding unit-cost inflation, delivery-year capex remaining controlled, sustained debt reduction after dividends/buybacks, and margin expansion sufficient to clear the 8.4% reverse growth bar without years of unusually high incremental returns. **What would weaken it:** the cash-flow advantage disappearing when deliveries resume, deposit growth reversing, or earnings per share improving mainly through refinancing/share-count effects.\n\nFor both, fuel, currency, refunds, environmental/port charges, tax changes, destination concentration, consumer spending and shipyard timing remain material. Deposits create funding resilience in normal conditions but can reverse during cancellations; a balance-sheet claim and cash-flow stress must not be counted twice.\n\n## Stage 3 versus Stage 3.5\n\nThe completed fundamental work does not clear either name at the quoted price under the base return hurdle. CCL deserves the earlier re-underwriting check because its value reaches the quote around a 9% WACC; RCL remains above the shown base sensitivities even there. Lower quotes or stronger evidenced cash economics can change either conclusion. No fixed discount percentage is imposed.\n\nA valid technical 3:1 uses **(credible target − actual entry) / (actual entry − technically justified stop)** and incorporates execution/slippage risk. It does not use this DCF's bear scenario as a stop, and a 3:1 chart setup cannot by itself resolve a failed fundamental valuation. Verify current market/sector conditions and price structure separately before considering promotion or a trade.\n\n## Reproducibility\n\nThe accompanying Python model and JSON contain every numerical assumption, each annual cash flow, each present value, and the full claim bridge. No trades were placed. Source and model uncertainty are disclosed as assumptions rather than being called unfinished research. Formal market data/consensus feeds were not used; price references are explicitly scanner-supplied.\n\n\n## Full dossier and reproducible model\n\n- [Open the full CCL dossier](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/stocks/CCL.html)\n- [Download the full report](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/CCL/report.md)\n- [Model inputs and annual forecasts](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/CCL/model.json)\n- [Reproducible Python model](https://fundamentalpullback.github.io/research/2026-11-04/CCL/model.py)\n","profiles":[{"ticker":"CCL","company":"Carnival Corporation & plc","kind":"complete","completedAt":"2026-11-04","label":"Stage 3 completo","summary":"Stage 3 completo; esperar precio. Candidata más cercana en valoración, pero 29,16 USD supera el central 22,41 USD con WACC 10%. Con 9% casi justifica el precio: requiere aceptar ese retorno y verificar reinversión. No hay entrada aprobada.","summaryEn":"Stage 3 complete; wait for price. The nearer valuation watch candidate, but $29.16 exceeds the $22.41 central case at 10% WACC. At 9% it nearly supports the price: that return hurdle and reinvestment need explicit underwriting. No approved entry.","valuation":{"status":"conditional","currency":"USD","asOf":"2026-11-04","bear":5.13,"base":22.41,"bull":41.12,"label":"Modelo FCFF a 10 años · supuestos explícitos","labelEn":"10-year FCFF model · explicit assumptions"}}]}].forEach(function(r){if(!seen.has(r.id)){window.FP_LIB.reports.push(r);seen.add(r.id);}});})();
